# Benchmark suite: generated binaries, hyperfine exports, rendered reports.
# Per-language *source* files under benchmarks/cases/ ARE tracked; only the
# build/run output is ignored. Plus stray native-compiler intermediates.
benchmarks/results/bin/
benchmarks/results/hf/
benchmarks/cases/**/*.hi
benchmarks/cases/**/*.cmi
benchmarks/cases/**/*.cmx
benchmarks/cases/**/*.o

# Written into the CWD by regression tests, which means they land at the repo
# root whenever the corpus is run from there. Both of the files below were
# COMMITTED once, in the same change that added the tests writing them — a
# corpus run then leaves the tree dirty, and a stale copy is a test input
# nobody wrote on purpose.
#   file_io_json_workflow.test.osp -> test_output.txt, test_stale_reason.txt
#   http_state_levels.test.osp     -> osprey_http_state_levels.db
# Leading `/` anchors these to the repo root the corpus actually writes to.
# Unanchored, `test_output.txt` also matched
# tests/regressions/basics/files/test_output.txt, reaching into the regression
# corpus and hiding any future file that happens to share the name.
/test_output.txt
/test_stale_reason.txt
/osprey_http_state_levels.db
# `osprey test <dir>` runs each program from its own directory, so the same two
# files also land beside the test. Fully anchored for the reason above: these
# exact paths, never a bare name that could hide a real fixture elsewhere.
/tests/regressions/basics/files/test_output.txt
/tests/regressions/basics/files/test_stale_reason.txt
/tests/regressions/effects/osprey_http_state_levels.db
